If I were to quote you as saying, "I love muffins.", the person that "I"
refers to in that is you, because within the quote, you are the speaker. So
yes, "I" and "you" do switch.

In the sentence, "You said it to me earlier.", the person being addressed is
"You", and the speaker is "me", obviously.

If it were, "You said to me, 'I like muffins.', earlier.", both "You"and "I"
refer to you, because within the context of the muffin quote, the speaker is
you, while outside the audience is you.

